course: dictionary
lesson: 1533
title: Certainly and sure words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Certainly means without any doubt, so the speaker is sure. «The map certainly shows the way to the lake.» The word tells you the writer is confident the map is right.
explain: In everyday talk, certainly is a polite way of saying yes. If someone asks, «Can I borrow a pencil?», you can answer, «Certainly.» It means "yes, of course".
explain: Look at the words around certainly. «Almost certainly» means very probably, but not completely sure, as in «Anyone who asks you to pay in gift cards is almost certainly a scammer.» «Certainly not» is a firm no. To check a meaning, swap in surely or of course and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Certainly means without any doubt; surely.
fact: As a polite answer, «Certainly» means «yes» or «of course».
fact: «Almost certainly» means very probably, but not completely sure.
fact: «Certainly» can also mean «it is true that…», often before a «but», as in «It is certainly fast, but it is not safe».
fact: «Certainly not» is a firm no.
exercise: «Can I borrow a pencil?» «Certainly.» What does certainly mean here? | Maybe later | No, never | Only a little | *Yes, of course
exercise: Alice says the bottle was «which certainly was not here before». What does certainly show? | Alice is asking a question | *Alice is sure the bottle was not here before | Alice thinks the bottle was here, but she has forgotten | Alice is angry about the bottle
exercise: Which phrase means very probably, but not completely sure? | Certainly | Certainly not | *Almost certainly | Certain to be lost
test: Anyone who asks you to read them a gift card code is almost certainly a scammer. What does almost certainly mean? | *Very probably a scammer | A friend who is sure to help | Definitely a scammer, with no doubt at all | Not a scammer at all
test: Ella asks, «Can I have a biscuit?» Her mum replies, «Certainly not!» What does this show? | A promise to buy some later | A question about biscuits | A polite yes | *A firm no
exam: Which word means "of course" when someone asks you for something? | Probably | Almost certainly | *Certainly | Certainly not
